## What a native tuple can't do yet (ADR 0829): each is a not-in-the-subset
## report, never a wrong program. Map keys and set elements need tuple keys
## in the tables (ADR 0568); a `char` has no quoted text form here; a table
## in a tuple would be shared by a copy.
fn main()
    m := Map[(int, int), int]()
    print(m.len())
    s := Set[(int, int)]()
    print(s.len())
    n := {(1, 2): 3}
    print(n.len())
    print((1, 'a'))
    t := (1, {"a": 2})
    print(t.0)
